  "text": "**Solution 1 is wrong.** Blow up a directed triangle: three classes V₁→V₂→V₃→V₁, all edges between consecutive classes oriented forward, with each vertex v given a private colour on its out-edges. Every vertex sees its in-part's colours plus one out-colour, so δ^c = ⌊n/3⌋+1. A 4-cycle has two forward and two backward steps (since 4 steps must sum to 0 mod 3). For it to be rainbow, each of its four vertices must own exactly one cycle edge, which forces a consistently directed cycle. Directed cycles here have length divisible by 3, so no rainbow C₄ exists. For n = 3k, this gives δ^c = k+1 = (n+3)/3 with no rainbow C₄, so (n+1)/3 fails.\n\n**Solution 2 is wrong.** Its C₅ construction gives δ^c only about n/5. The three-class construction above gives δ^c ≈ n/3 with no rainbow C₄, and (n+6)/5 < n/3 for n > 9, so the proposed bound cannot force a rainbow C₄.\n\n**Solution 3 is the only one consistent with the checks I can make.** Its form (n+a)/3 matches the three-class construction. That construction forces a ≥ 4 (at n ≡ 0 mod 3 the threshold must be k+2). The value a = 5 is plausible, but I could not verify the cited Czygrinow–Molla–Nagle–Oursler result or rule out a = 6 (which differs from a = 5 only when n ≡ 1 mod 3). So this is a best-supported choice, not a confirmed one.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
